---
import Breadcrumbs from './Breadcrumbs.astro';
import TokenizerDemo from './TokenizerDemo.astro';
import NextSteps from './NextSteps.astro';
---

<!-- Breadcrumbs -->
<Breadcrumbs items={[
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Core Concepts', href: '/core-concepts' },
  { label: 'Tokenization' },
]} />

<!-- Hero -->
<header class="mb-12">
  <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-violet" />
    <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-violet">Core Concepts</span>
  </span>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Tokenization
  </h1>
  <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
    Words become subwords become numbers. Tokenization is the crucial first step
    that transforms human language into something a neural network can actually process.
  </p>
</header>

<!-- Section: The big picture -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    The big picture: words → subwords → numbers
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      A neural network can't process text directly — it only understands numbers. Tokenization
      is the translation layer between human language and machine computation. It takes raw
      text and converts it into a sequence of integer IDs that the model can work with.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of it like this: you hand the model the sentence "The cat sleeps" and it needs
      to receive something like <code class="text-blue-violet">[1234, 5678, 9012]</code>.
      The tokenizer is the dictionary that makes this translation possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      But it's not as simple as mapping whole words to numbers. That approach fails on
      unfamiliar words, creates impossibly large vocabularies, and can't handle languages
      without clear word boundaries. Modern tokenizers use <strong>subword</strong>
      tokenization — breaking words into meaningful pieces that balance expressiveness
      with vocabulary size.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: Why tokenization matters -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Why tokenization matters
  </h2>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #5E5CE6; background-color: #5E5CE612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2" />
          <path d="M9 9h6v6H9z" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Determines what the model can express
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          If a word isn't in the vocabulary, the tokenizer must split it into pieces.
          Common words like "the" get their own token, but rare words like "antidisestablishmentarianism"
          break into smaller parts: ["anti", "dis", "establish", "ment", "arian", "ism"].
          This means the model understands rare words by composing known pieces.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #7B79D6; background-color: #7B79D612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M13 2L9 14 13 6 9 22" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Affects model size and speed
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Vocabulary size directly impacts the embedding layer — the model's first layer
          that converts token IDs to vectors. A 50,000-token vocabulary means 50,000
          embedding vectors. More tokens per input also means longer sequences to process,
          slowing down generation. Tokenizer design is a constant tradeoff.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #9896C6; background-color: #9896C612;">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
          <path d="M2 12h20" />
          <path d="M12 2a15.3 15.3 0 014 10 15.3 15.3 0 01-4 10 15.3 15.3 0 01-4-10 15.3 15.3 0 014-10z" />
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Enables cross-lingual understanding
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Subword tokenizers handle multiple languages naturally. The pieces "com", "put",
          and "er" appear in English "computer", French "ordinateur" (partial match),
          and Spanish "computadora". This shared vocabulary helps models transfer knowledge
          across languages without explicit translation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: How BPE works -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    How Byte Pair Encoding works
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4 mb-6">
    <p>
      Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) is the most widely used subword tokenization algorithm.
      It was originally designed for compressing text, but researchers discovered it
      produces excellent tokenization for natural language.
    </p>
    <p>
      The algorithm works in two phases: <strong>training</strong> and <strong>tokenization</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
    <!-- Phase 1 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          1
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Training: Build the merge table
        </h3>
      </div>
      <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
        <p>
          Start with every character as its own token. Then repeatedly find the most
          frequent pair of adjacent tokens and merge them into a new token.
        </p>
        <p>
          For example, if "l" + "o" appears most often, merge them into "lo". Then if
          "lo" + " " appears most often, merge into "lo ". Keep going until the
          vocabulary reaches the target size (e.g., 50,000 tokens).
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 p-3 rounded-md bg-gray-50 font-mono text-xs text-gray-600">
          <div>Start: "l", "o", "w", "e", "r"</div>
          <div>Merge "l"+"o" → "lo"</div>
          <div>Merge "l"+"o" → "lo" (in "lower")</div>
          <div>Merge "lo"+"w" → "low"</div>
          <div>Merge "low"+"e" → "lowe"</div>
          <div>Merge "lowe"+"r" → "lower"</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Phase 2 -->
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-blue-violet text-white text-xs font-bold">
          2
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black">
          Tokenization: Split new text
        </h3>
      </div>
      <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
        <p>
          When tokenizing new text, split it into characters first, then greedily apply
          the merge rules from training. Always apply the most specific (longest) match
          available.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 p-3 rounded-md bg-gray-50 font-mono text-xs text-gray-600">
          <div>Input: "playing"</div>
          <div>Split: "p", "l", "a", "y", "i", "n", "g"</div>
          <div>Apply merges: "play" + "ing"</div>
          <div>Result: ["play", "ing"]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
    <p>
      This is why "playing" tokenizes as <code class="text-blue-violet">["play", "ing"]</code>
      — the training data showed that "play" and "ing" are both useful tokens on their own,
      but "playing" never appeared frequently enough to earn its own token.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Interactive demo -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-2">
    Try it yourself
  </h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-6">
    Type any text below to see how a simplified BPE tokenizer breaks it into subword tokens.
    Each colored block shows one token with its vocabulary ID. Click the preset buttons
    to try common examples, or type your own text.
  </p>
  <TokenizerDemo containerClass="mb-6" />
</section>

<!-- Section: Tokenizer comparison -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Other tokenization approaches
  </h2>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
        WordPiece
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Used by BERT. Similar to BPE but picks merges by
        maximizing likelihood instead of frequency.
      </p>
      <span class="inline-block text-xs font-mono text-blue-violet bg-blue-violet/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">
        Google BERT
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
        Unigram
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Starts with a large candidate set and prunes tokens
        to optimize a scoring function. Used by SentencePiece.
      </p>
      <span class="inline-block text-xs font-mono text-blue-violet bg-blue-violet/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">
        SentencePiece
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-2">
        Byte-level BPE
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Operates on UTF-8 bytes instead of Unicode characters.
        Handles any text without unknown tokens.
      </p>
      <span class="inline-block text-xs font-mono text-blue-violet bg-blue-violet/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">
        GPT, LLaMA
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: Key takeaways -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Key takeaways
  </h2>
  <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6">
    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Tokenization converts text to numbers so neural networks can process it</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Subword tokenization handles unknown words by composing known pieces</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>BPE builds its vocabulary by merging the most frequent token pairs</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>Vocabulary size trades off between expressiveness and model efficiency</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2">
        <span class="flex-shrink-0 text-blue-violet">→</span>
        <span>One word can become multiple tokens — "unbelievable" might be 4-5 tokens</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

